            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Urdu for in agreement, then the Urdu for a side in a dispute."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ثابت کرنا",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ثابت کرنا — sābit karnā — \"to prove\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "آپ اپنی بات کیسے ثابت کریں گے؟ āp apnī bāt kaise sābit kareṅ ge? How will you prove what you say?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Past, with نے (he or she …): اس نے اپنی بات ثابت کی, us ne apnī bāt sābit kī."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ثابت کرنا (sābit karnā) is built from sābit, usually traced, through Persian, to Arabic thābit, \"firm, established\", and کرنا, \"to do\": to make a claim stand firm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for giving reasons and opinions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
